"""Invoice narrative grounded in the user's earlier case study; proposals are explicit."""
from html import escape as e
from portfolio_layout import render, contact_cta

def invoice_case(project, next_project):
    def figure(file, caption):
        return f'''<figure class="case-figure"><button class="case-image" type="button" data-image="/reference/profile/invoice/{file}.png" data-title="Invoice creation — {e(caption)}" aria-label="Enlarge invoice design"><img src="/reference/profile/invoice/{file}.png" alt="{e(caption)}" loading="lazy"><span>take a closer look ↗</span></button><figcaption>{e(caption)}</figcaption></figure>'''
    content = f'''<div class="section-rule"><a href="/projects">← all work</a><span>[Karbon / invoice creation]</span></div>
<header class="case-header invoice-header"><p class="eyebrow">FINTECH · PRODUCT DESIGN · 2024</p><h1 class="sticker">Invoices, without<br>the back-and-forth.</h1><p class="case-subtitle">Redesigning invoice creation for Indian exporters</p><p class="invoice-deck">Users were creating invoices elsewhere, uploading PDFs to Karbon and sharing payment details separately. This redesign brings those disconnected tasks into a guided flow.</p><dl class="case-facts"><div><dt>My focus</dt><dd>Invoice creation, interaction design and review experience</dd></div><div><dt>Product</dt><dd>Karbon · cross-border payments</dd></div><div><dt>Year</dt><dd>2024</dd></div></dl></header>
<figure class="case-hero"><img src="/reference/profile/invoice/overview.png" alt="Karbon invoice creation design overview" fetchpriority="high"></figure>
<nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Case study chapters"><a href="#brief-title">At a glance</a><a href="#problem">The problem</a><a href="#research">Research</a><a href="#decisions">Design decisions</a><a href="#prototype">Interaction example</a><a href="#iteration">Iteration plan</a><a href="#outcomes">Outcomes</a></nav>
<section class="case-brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title"><h2 id="brief-title">The project at a glance</h2><dl><div><dt>The problem</dt><dd>A fragmented workflow and a dense form made creating an invoice inside Karbon harder than using an external tool.</dd></div><div><dt>My design contribution</dt><dd>The guided creation flow: payment intent, form structure, split-screen review, purpose-code grouping and reminder setup.</dd></div><div><dt>The design output</dt><dd>A step-by-step experience that connects invoice creation and review, with reminder scheduling at the end.</dd></div></dl><details class="evidence-note"><summary>Scope and evidence notes</summary><p>The project story and screenshots come from my earlier portfolio. This page describes my design contribution; it does not claim that I alone owned research, engineering or business outcomes. Exact team responsibilities, launch status and measurement context still need to be documented.</p></details></section>
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE WORKAROUND</p><h2>Getting paid meant moving between tools.</h2><div class="workflow-comparison"><div><h3>Previous workflow</h3><ol><li>Create an invoice in an external tool</li><li>Upload the PDF to Karbon</li><li>Enter payment information again</li><li>Share it and chase payment manually</li></ol></div><div><h3>Redesigned workflow</h3><ol><li>Choose the payment request type</li><li>Add customer and payment details</li><li>Review the invoice alongside the form</li><li>Configure reminders before finishing</li></ol></div></div><p class="chapter-copy">The problem was more than a long form. Users had to switch between commercial information, unfamiliar purpose codes and the question of what their customer would actually receive.</p></section>
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="research"><p class="eyebrow">02 / WHAT INFORMED THE DESIGN</p><h2>One workflow. Three different priorities.</h2><div class="user-needs"><article><h3>Freelancers</h3><p>A simple way to request payment without unnecessary invoice setup.</p></article><article><h3>Finance teams</h3><p>Accurate invoice details and a clearer place to handle purpose-code information.</p></article><article><h3>Operations leads</h3><p>Recurring requests and reminders that reduce repetitive follow-up work.</p></article></div><p class="chapter-copy">The earlier case study describes 15 user interviews and three recurring themes: invoices being created elsewhere, uncertainty around purpose codes, and manual follow-ups. Those themes informed the flow below.</p><details class="evidence-note"><summary>Research documentation to add</summary><p>Add the interview period, participant split, recruitment method and anonymized notes. The previously reported 68% abandonment and 73% external-PDF usage also need an analytics source, event definitions and observation window before they can serve as verified evidence.</p></details></section>
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="decisions"><p class="eyebrow">03 / THE DESIGN REASONING</p><h2>Four decisions that connect the flow.</h2><ol class="invoice-decisions"><li><div><span>01</span><h3>Ask what the user wants to send.</h3></div><p>One-time invoices, recurring invoices and payment links serve different needs. Establishing intent first gives the rest of the flow a clearer starting point.</p></li><li><div><span>02</span><h3>Let people review while they create.</h3></div><p>The split screen puts the document beside the inputs. It gives users a visible reference for checking customer details and amounts before they finish, rather than discovering the document only after submission.</p></li><li><div><span>03</span><h3>Separate payment details from purpose codes.</h3></div><p>Grouping amounts and regulatory information into different steps reduces the number of unrelated decisions competing on one screen. Moving the field alone does not explain it; the next iteration should test contextual guidance as well.</p></li><li><div><span>04</span><h3>Make follow-up part of setup.</h3></div><p>Reminder scheduling appears at the end of the flow, with options around the due date. This addresses the manual chasing described by operations users without interrupting invoice entry.</p></li></ol></section>
{figure('flow','Original design: payment-intent selection beside an invoice preview.')}
{figure('details','Original design artifact from the invoice flow. Click to inspect the interface.')}
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="prototype"><p class="eyebrow">04 / A CLOSER LOOK AT THE INTERACTION</p><h2>The preview should match the request.</h2><p class="chapter-copy">This proposed refinement corrects the customer label and one-time-invoice wording. It also changes the preview when a payment link is selected. Try changing the request type or customer name.</p><p class="sample-label">INTERACTIVE EXPLORATION · SAMPLE DATA · NOT A SHIPPED PRODUCT</p>
<div class="invoice-demo" data-invoice-demo><div class="demo-inputs"><h3>How would you like to request money?</h3><label for="demo-customer">Customer</label><input id="demo-customer" value="Example Studio" maxlength="60" autocomplete="off"><fieldset><legend>Request type</legend><label><input type="radio" name="request-type" value="one-time" checked><span>Create a one-time invoice<small>An invoice with a payment link.</small></span></label><label><input type="radio" name="request-type" value="recurring"><span>Create a recurring series<small>A recurring invoice with a monthly schedule.</small></span></label><label><input type="radio" name="request-type" value="link"><span>Request money without an invoice<small>A payment request, without a formal invoice.</small></span></label></fieldset><p class="demo-note">Exploration only. Nothing is created or sent.</p></div><section class="demo-preview" aria-label="Request preview"><p class="demo-kicker">PREVIEW · SAMPLE DATA</p><h3 data-preview-title>Invoice</h3><dl><div><dt>To</dt><dd data-preview-customer>Example Studio</dd></div><div><dt>Amount</dt><dd>USD 1,200.00</dd></div><div data-invoice-only><dt>Invoice</dt><dd>DEMO-001</dd></div><div data-schedule hidden><dt>Schedule</dt><dd>Monthly</dd></div></dl><p data-preview-copy>One-time invoice for design services.</p><span class="demo-payment">Payment link preview</span><p data-preview-status class="demo-note" aria-live="polite">Showing a one-time invoice preview.</p></section></div><p class="chapter-copy">On smaller screens, the preview follows the controls in the same reading order. A production version still needs testing for error recovery, long customer names and the complete multi-step journey.</p><noscript><p>The static example shows a one-time invoice. Enable JavaScript to switch request types.</p></noscript></section>
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="iteration"><p class="eyebrow">05 / FROM DESIGN TO EVIDENCE</p><h2>The next iteration to validate.</h2><p class="chapter-copy">The existing material shows the solution, but does not document a tested iteration. This is a proposed test plan—not a claim that these sessions already happened.</p><div class="iteration-plan"><article><h3>Question</h3><p>Do users understand why a payment-link request does not produce an invoice?</p></article><article><h3>Proposed revision</h3><p>Change the preview title and remove invoice-only fields when that option is selected.</p></article><article><h3>What to observe</h3><p>Ask users to choose a request type and explain what the customer will receive. Record incorrect choices, hesitation and whether they notice the preview change.</p></article></div><p class="evidence-pending">To complete this story: add an actual test observation, the design revision it prompted and the result of retesting.</p></section>
<section class="invoice-chapter" id="outcomes"><p class="eyebrow">06 / OUTCOMES &amp; MEASUREMENT</p><h2>What the design changes—and what needs evidence.</h2><p class="chapter-copy">The design brings payment intent, invoice review and reminders into a single flow. A quantified improvement needs a defined metric and a comparable before-and-after measurement.</p><details class="evidence-note"><summary>Previously reported results · measurement context pending</summary><div class="metric-table-wrap"><table class="metric-review"><thead><tr><th>Earlier claim</th><th>What it means</th><th>Evidence to add</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adoption: 22% → 77%</td><td>+55 percentage points; 3.5× the starting rate.</td><td>Definition of adoption, eligible-user denominator, cohorts and dates.</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance tickets: −65%</td><td>A reported reduction in a ticket category.</td><td>Ticket taxonomy, comparable periods and adjustment for customer volume.</td></tr><tr><td>Support savings: ~$45K/year</td><td>A reported annual cost estimate.</td><td>Ticket volumes, handling time, cost assumptions and attribution.</td></tr><tr><td>CSAT: 62% → 89%</td><td>+27 percentage points.</td><td>Survey question, respondent counts and collection periods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are claims from the earlier portfolio, not independently verified results. They are retained here for documentation, rather than presented as established impact. The undefined “80% improvement” headline has been removed.</p></details><p class="chapter-copy">The earlier customer testimonial and broad competitor compliance claims are omitted until their source and context can be confirmed.</p></section>
<section class="next-project"><div><p class="eyebrow">[next story]</p><h2 class="handwritten">{e(next_project['title'])}</h2></div><a class="button button-yellow" href="/projects/{e(next_project['slug'])}">Next case study ↗</a></section>{contact_cta()}'''
    return render(project['case_title'], project['summary'], content, 'work', 'case-page invoice-case')
